A||N||17||Monkeyman||Building the perfect box||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||10-31-2001||06:23 PM||||I'm trying to build a ported sub box in the front compartment but I'm having some trouble figuring the dimensions.  It needs to be 1.62 ft<sup>3</sup>.  I'd like to build an amp rack on top of this, too.  Can anyone help me, please??!?  I'd like to get this done before the snow flies.  I've looked at some basic (DOS) programs but they aren't any help.  I wanted to be able to keep the space for the sunroof storage, but I never remove that so I can use all of the space up to the hood.<P>Any help would be greatly appreciated.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PontiacAPE.jpg">    <BR>Monkeyman||||reg||5
Z||000001||Sootah||10-31-2001||06:39 PM||||I'd go with the behind the seat enclosures that <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierosound/" TARGET=_blank>FieroSound</A> did. Won't be ported but in a cabin as small as the Fiero's it hardly matters. 'Sides the subs being up front where your talking about will just rattle more stuff and make the bass 'dirty'.<P>I was actually programming a subwoofer enclosure dealie a while ago, but after my friend went to NJ (mission, we're Mormons) I kinda lost interest. I know a lot more than most people, but he's the real audiophile.<P>Anyway, I'll see if I can remember how to calculate that for you. Give me the equasion if you can remember it. (I can't been out o school for too blasted long)<P><B><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierosound/" TARGET=_blank>FieroSound's Site</A></B><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Nuclear_Happy_Face_Sig.jpg">     <BR><sup><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I></sup> <BR><sup><B>AIM</B>: KSSouter</sup>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||11-01-2001||01:18 AM||||I was originally going to mount the sub in a sealed enclosure behind the passenger seat, but now I've got other plans for the interior.  I'm definitely going to do a ported front compartment box.  I just have to figure out how to do it right.<P>I'll see if I can come up with the T/S numbers tomorrow (at work now) for ya.  According to the paperwork that came with the sub, it's supposed to have a 1.62 ft<sup>3</sup> ported enclosure.  I tried to measure the front compartment, but I'm not even sure what dimensions would add up to that figure.  I'm going to try to add an amp rack to the top of the sub box so I'll need something a little wider and slightly shorter than most would do.  Email me and maybe you can help me out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||sloth85GT||11-01-2001||02:06 AM||||Mach10 has a sub enclosure that fits by the passengers feet. He made it himself, sounds REAL nice. He's gonna be making me one soon too. They are inexpensive and easy to install and sound really good, just uses a bit of foot room that's all. Doesn't come out too far and fits one 10"er.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.mts.net/~kuzma/fieronl.gif">     <BR>Dan<BR>1985 Silver GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||Joe Torma||11-01-2001||02:22 AM||||The magic number you may be looking for is 1728.  12"x12"x12"=1728cuin=1cuft.<P>So measure in inches...length, width and height of the area you're trying to stuff the box in and divide by 1728...you'll get the cu ft of the area.<P>Now, remember, the walls have a thickness and the speaker displaces some area too.  You should build this into the area of the box.<P>Build the box a bit smaller for punchy tight bass.  Larger for looser, noiser bass.  You may not notice it with some speakers.<P>Stay withing +/- 10% of the given area.<P>I don't like ported enclosures.  Limited frequency responce.  You may gain 3db from having it in one though.<P>Bandpass if built right will slam!  But freq response is still limited.<P>But I like good ol sealed boxes.<P>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig1.gif">  <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR> <A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A>||||reg||4
Z||000005||Monkeyman||11-01-2001||07:26 AM||||sloth85GT--One of the reasons I'm going with a ported box in the front compartment is for asthetics.  I have some changes taking place in the interior and I just don't want a sub there unless it's hidden.  Also, leg room is one of the (many) things I love about a Fiero.  I plan on some longer trips once I get a new motor (next year?) so my wife needs to be able to stretch out.  Also, my best friend rides with me once in a while.  He's 6'7" tall with a long inseam.<P>Joe Torma--Thanx for the figure.  That's one of the things I was looking for.  Now I can figure one or two dimensions and see what the 3<sup>rd</sup> needs to be.  I remembered to allow for the volume that the sub (and port) displace, but I forgot about allowing for the thickness of the wood.  My sub tends to have a looser,  noisier sound to it anyway, so I'll probably build the box slightly smaller (maybe 0.25 ft<sup>3</sup>) just to get it "right".  I've had it in a sealed enclosure (one of Aus') already.  It sounded nice, but like I said, it just doesn't belong in the interior any longer.<P>It's been suggested that I try a bandpass box, but a simple ported box is confusing enough.  With all of the "orders" of bandpass boxes (what the heck are "orders" anyway?), I'd get even more confused.<P>----------------------<P>Here's another question.  Do I mount the sub on one of the walls of the box?  If so, is there a certain wall that would work better?  Or be worse?<P>Thanx again for your help so far.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Joe Torma||11-02-2001||01:34 AM||||Pointing the sub in any direction will do the same job...rattle the heck out of whatever is in front of it.<P>Go bandpass.  You are building a small enough enclosure it seems so build it twice as big, split it in half put the speaker in the divider and port the one end.  Well its kinda that easy.<P>Take and post some measurements as well as the sub's requirements.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||11-02-2001||05:34 AM||||OK.  Here goes...I have a 10" Cerwin Vega Stealth series sub.  Model S-10.4.  I'm not sure what all of these mean, but I'll post them anyway.<P>V box rear=1.00 cu ft (net) + 0.035 cu ft (driver displacement)<P>V box front=0.50 cu ft (net) + port displacement<P>Tuning Frequency:  61 Hz<P>Port Diameter (3" circular):<BR>--Port Area:  7.06 sq in<BR>--Number or ports: 1<BR>--Port Length:  8.30"<BR>--Port Displacement:  58.00 cu in/0.030 cu ft<P>Sensitivity:  93 dB<BR>Power Handling:  (cont. unclipped power per EIA RS-426C):  200 Wrms (nominal)/400 Wrms (max)<BR>Frequency response:  40-500 Hz<BR>Nominal Impedance:  4 (can't do the symbol but I think it's Ohms)<BR>Mounting dia: 9"<BR>Mounting Depth:  4 1/16"<P>S/T Parameters<P>Free-Air Resonance:  fs  41 Hz<BR>Total Q:  Qts  0.486<BR>Mechanical Q:  Qms  7.93<BR>Electrical Q:  Qes  0.517<BR>Compliance Volume:  Vas  1.26 cu ft<BR>Maximum Excursion:  Xmax peak  +/-0.313"<BR>Voice Coil DC Resistance:  Revc  3.4 Ohms<BR>Driver Displacement:  103.70 cu in<P>(All of that's out of the owners manual for the sub.  There's also a Suspension Bandpass Frequency Response graph, but my scanner's dead.  If you need any info off of that, LMK.)<P>I'm not sure of the dimensions of the front compartment.  I have no problem removing the spare tire, jack, etc (already have).  If I remove the plastic spare tire floor (don't know what it's really called), will that allow water, dirt, etc to get into the front compartment?  If not, I'd be more than willing to remove that, too.  LMK if I can remove that, and I will, then do some measuring.<P>Thanx.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Phil||11-02-2001||06:26 AM||||You could always put a big plastic bag in the front and fill it with those foam packing peanuts , then dump the peanuts into a a cardboard box and measure the volume||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||11-02-2001||06:35 AM||||Good idea, Phil.||||reg||1
